    "result_text": "There's only one test class in the project, and it passes, along with a full `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec asks that a successful owner create emit an AUDIT log line carrying the owner id, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate`.\n\n**Change:** one file, one existing terminal step \u2014 `RespondWithOwnerCreated.java` (the final `respond` step of `owners.POST.yml`, which already runs only on a successful create and has the fully-populated `owner` in hand):\n\n```java\nLoggerFactory.getLogger(\"AUDIT\").info(\"owner created id={} customerCode={} registrationDate={}\",\n        owner.getId(), owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate());\n```\n\nplus the `org.slf4j.LoggerFactory` import.\n\n**Why this placement (structural impact):** all three fields are on `owner` at the responder, so no new state plumbing is needed. That class has a single method (WMC_other = 0), the method stays CC = 1 (no new branches), and the edit is 3 lines in **one** file \u2014 no new function class and no new YAML step, which would have pushed the file-count multiplier to 2. This is the minimum-cost point that still fires exactly on success. The AUDIT output confirms it: `AUDIT - owner created id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-04`.",
